## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire request into memory by default. For large files, read directly from `Request.Body` or use `Request.ReadFormAsync` with buffering disabled.

### Approach: Stream multipart body to disk manually

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities;
using Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Raise the max request body size (default is ~28MB)
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpContext context) =>
{
    // Disable request body buffering so data streams through
    context.Features.Get<IHttpBodyControlFeature>()!.AllowSynchronousIO = false;

    var contentType = context.Request.ContentType;
    if (contentType is null || !contentType.Contains("multipart/"))
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart request.");

    var boundary = HeaderUtilities.RemoveQuotes(
        MediaTypeHeaderValue.Parse(contentType).Boundary).Value;

    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(boundary))
        return Results.BadRequest("Missing boundary.");

    var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, context.Request.Body);
    var section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();

    while (section is not null)
    {
        var hasDisposition = ContentDispositionHeaderValue
            .TryParse(section.ContentDisposition, out var disposition);

        if (hasDisposition && disposition!.IsFileDisposition())
        {
            // Sanitize filename
            var fileName = Path.GetRandomFileName() +
                Path.GetExtension(disposition.FileName.Value);
            var savePath = Path.Combine("/tmp/uploads", fileName);

            Directory.CreateDirectory("/tmp/uploads");

            // Stream directly to disk — no full-file memory buffer
            await using var targetStream = File.Create(savePath);
            await section.Body.CopyToAsync(targetStream);

            return Results.Ok(new { file = fileName, size = targetStream.Length });
        }

        section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();
    }

    return Results.BadRequest("No file found in request.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery(); // Required for minimal APIs accepting form uploads without tokens

app.Run();
```

### Key points

| Setting | Why |
|---------|-----|
| `MaxRequestBodySize = 2GB` | Kestrel rejects bodies above 28 MB by default |
| `MultipartReader` | Reads one section at a time from the raw stream |
| `CopyToAsync` | Copies chunks (default 80 KB) to disk — constant memory |
| No `[FromForm]` / `IFormFile` | These trigger full buffering via `FormOptions.MemoryBufferThreshold` |

### If you're behind IIS/HTTP.sys

Add this to `web.config`:
```xml
<system.webServer>
  <security>
    <requestFiltering>
      <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="2147483648" />
    </requestFiltering>
  </security>
</system.webServer>
```

### Optional: disable form buffering globally

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<FormOptions>(options =>
{
    options.MultipartBodyLengthLimit = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024;
});
```

This only matters if other endpoints still use `IFormFile`; the streaming approach above bypasses `FormOptions` entirely.
